# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# What must pass before main takes a merge. github-guard reads this from the
# default branch on the server and applies it to branch protection.
#
#   ci-ok   ci.yml: the one always-run gate. It `needs:` every other job in
#           that workflow and fails when any of them failed, was cancelled or
#           was SKIPPED -- today that is the `test` matrix on ubuntu and
#           macOS: gofmt, vet, the race-enabled suite, the build, and the
#           curated examples run against the built binary on both, plus
#           actionlint, ShellCheck, the manual-in-sync check and this gate
#           on the ubuntu leg.
#
# WHY ONE NAME AND NOT TWO (#54). This list used to be the two matrix legs,
# `test (ubuntu-latest)` and `test (macos-latest)`, typed into the settings
# page and written down nowhere in this repository. Both were correct when
# they were typed, and that is the problem -- nothing kept them correct. A
# matrix leg's check name is exactly the name that moves: adding a Linux
# arm64 leg produces a check that reports on every pull request and gates
# nothing, and renaming the job, or giving it a `name:`, turns both required
# names into contexts no job produces.
#
# The opposite spelling is worse. A required context that no job produces
# reads to GitHub as permanently pending, and with enforce_admins on nothing
# merges and there is no failure to point at. Both directions come from the
# required list naming individual jobs rather than one gate, and both have
# happened in this constellation.
#
# THIS REPOSITORY SHIPS THE RULE, so it is the last one that can be exempt
# from it. `chore ci:gate` is how the siblings are told to hold themselves to
# this, and a tool that does not apply its own rule is the weaker argument
# for adopting it.
#
# THE NAME DOES NOT MOVE WHEN THE JOBS DO, which is what makes this safe to
# change. github-guard reads this file from the DEFAULT BRANCH, so a pull
# request that renames a gating job can never also be the one whose
# protection learns the new name -- it would leave the old name required and
# never reporting. Requiring the aggregate means the jobs behind it can be
# renamed, split or replaced wholesale in a single pull request that
# protection never notices.
#
# ONLY ci.yml CAN GATE, WHICH IS WHY ci-ok LIVES THERE AND NEEDS ONLY ITS
# JOBS. release.yml triggers on a `v*` tag or by hand, after the merge it
# would be gating has already happened, so it never reports on a pull request.
# Requiring anything from it would require a check GitHub reads as pending
# forever -- a permanent block on every merge. It stays absent.
#
# scripts/ci-gate.sh HOLDS BOTH HALVES TO THIS -- every job in ci.yml has to
# be in `ci-ok`'s `needs`, and this file has to require `ci-ok` and nothing
# else. Without that the aggregate drifts exactly as the named list did: a
# job added to ci.yml and not to `needs` is invisible again. It runs on the
# ubuntu leg of `test`, and locally as `chore ci:gate`. The step after it
# compares this file with the live protection on main and WARNS rather than
# fails: a pull request cannot change a setting, so it must not be the thing
# that goes red when the two disagree.
#
# The name must match the check-run name GitHub reports, which is the job's
# `name:` rather than its key -- `ci-ok` for both.
[checks]
	required = ci-ok
